Git Hooks
*********

.[perex]
A pre-commit hook for PHP code style that stays silent while everything is clean and checks only what goes into the commit. For AI agents, see the page [AI Agents |agents].


Pre-commit
==========

A hook should be fast and quiet: check only the files that go into the commit, and print nothing when everything is fine. That is exactly what the `lines` format is for: one line per finding. The `--stdin` option also lets the hook check the content staged in the index, which may differ from the file in the working tree:

```shell
#!/bin/sh
status=0
for file in $(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM -- '*.php'); do
	git show ":$file" | dresscode check --stdin "$file" -f lines --skip-excluded || status=1
done
exit $status
```

Save it as `.git/hooks/pre-commit` and make it executable. When everything is fine, the hook prints nothing and the commit goes through; otherwise it prints each violation on one line with the path, the line, the message and the decision path, and stops the commit:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Cart.php:5:5: error: The import of `Gateway` is unused. [imports.unused]
src/Cart.php:15:21: error: A plain string must be written with single quotes. [literals.quotes]
\--

Thanks to `--skip-excluded`, the hook skips a file that the configuration excludes in `excludePaths` or that is outside its `paths`; without it, `--stdin` would check that file too.

If you want a hook that fixes right away, run `fix` over the working tree and add the fixed files to the index. It is convenient, but it means the commit contains changes the developer has not seen. I prefer the first variant: the hook only reports, and I run the fix on purpose.

With the `pre-commit` tool, it is one entry in the configuration. The tool passes file names to DressCode, so the entry needs `--skip-excluded`, otherwise it would also check a file that the configuration excludes in `excludePaths`:

```yaml
repos:
  - repo: local
    hooks:
      - id: dresscode
        name: DressCode
        entry: dresscode check -f lines --skip-excluded
        language: system
        types: [php]
```
